The actual market problem

Robinhood Stock Tokens are ERC-20 debt securities providing economic exposure to referenced shares; they are not the shares themselves. They use Chainlink prices and a multiplier to handle corporate actions.

The Stock Token contracts and pools remain accessible around the clock, even when the referenced equity market, authorised participant and hedging liquidity are unavailable. That mismatch creates:

Weekend price dislocations.
Toxic flow against passive LPs.
Thin-liquidity squeezes.
Stale reference prices.
Sudden repricing when issuance or traditional markets resume.

This is not hypothetical. On August 30, a Stock Token referencing Hims & Hers reportedly traded at $61.15 onchain after the underlying closed at $28.84. The premium collapsed after new supply arrived on Monday.

Robinhood’s API already exposes the raw underlying bid/ask, Stock Token multiplier, corporate-action information and whether an asset supports all-day trading. The onchain Chainlink price is multiplier-adjusted, so the two sources must be normalized correctly.

That gives you the ingredients for a defensible product.

How GapSafe would work
Trader experience

A user swaps NVDA/USDG normally. The interface displays:

NYSE: CLOSED
Current pool/reference divergence.
Oracle freshness.
Applicable dynamic fee.
Normal, guarded or stale-data mode.

